tic prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces; neutral to mildly basic pH; geothermal heating and cooling cycles.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lewis-acid-assisted hydration of the nitrile gives the corresponding amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis normally passes through the amide. Warm aqueous mineral interfaces and Fe2+-assisted activation make this intermediate explicit rather than hiding it inside a single net hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Non-enzymatic prebiotic pools undergoing repeated heating; near-neutral to mildly basic water; slower than nitrile hydration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amide hydrolysis yields the amino acid and releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a plausible but slower, potentially rate-limiting step. Prolonged geothermal cycling provides the harsher aqueous exposure needed for glycinamide to reach glycine without enzymes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Overall hydrothermal hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\", \"iron sulfide surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-430 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+ and iron sulfide surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Subseafloor hot water, neutral to mildly alkaline pH, prolonged residence in hot compressed water.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Overall nitrile hydration followed by amide hydrolysis under hot aqueous conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct overall conversion of aminoacetonitrile to glycine is chemically reasonable only under explicitly harsher aqueous conditions. Assigning this step to hydrothermal water with mineral Lewis acids provides the temperature, pH, and catalytic justification that direct hydrolysis requires.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-like hydantoin formation from formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-355 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Carbonate or bicarbonate-rich ammonium cyanide brines in evaporitic pools or hot-spring margins; repeated wet-dry cycles drive concentration and ring closure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-like sequence: aldehyde cyanohydrin or iminium chemistry is followed by carbamylation with CO2-derived carbonate species and cyclization to hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin formation is more condition-sensitive and less robust than the Strecker branch, so it is restricted here to explicit carbonate-rich drying environments rather than generic surface water. Under concentrated ammoniacal cyanide conditions, hydantoin is a plausible protected precursor to glycine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Carboxylative cyclization of glycinamide to hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-355 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n 