ments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.86,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.92,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.82,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.95,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.88,\n      \"feedback\": \"Strongest route in the network. Direct Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile from formaldehyde, HCN, and NH3 is chemically well grounded, and hydrothermal conversion of aminoacetonitrile to glycine is plausible. The main fixes are explicit stoichiometry for rxn_008, which should consume 2 H2O, and a clearer physical rationale for moving the aminonitrile from the surface setting into hot aqueous conditions.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Rewrite rxn_008 as mol_007 + 2 mol_001 -> mol_009 + mol_003\",\n        \"Specify hydrothermal temperature, pH, or mineral assistance for aminonitrile hydrolysis\",\n        \"Add a transport step or explanation linking the surface synthesis site to the hydrothermal aqueous environment\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.72,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.7\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.76,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.46,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.91,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"The chemistry is mechanistically sensible because nitrile hydrolysis commonly proceeds through an amide, but the stated environment is the major weakness. rxn_006 and rxn_007 are not convincingly supported as prebiotic 'Biochemical' steps unless they are reinterpreted as hot aqueous or mineral-catalyzed hydrolysis.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problema