{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T01:01:02.708372", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Glycine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.84\n- Feedback: The network is connected and mostly valid: all 11 molecules and 9 reactions belong to one component, IDs are consistent, and the RDKit check finds no unbalanceable reactions. Correct rxn_005 to aminoacetonitrile + 2 H2O -> glycine + NH3 and rxn_009 to hydantoin + 2 H2O -> glycine + NH3 + CO2. The Strecker manifolds are the strongest part of the network and plausibly reach glycine, with the best-supported route being the one-pot Strecker to aminoacetonitrile followed by hydrolysis via glycinamide. Confidence is limited by underspecified environments and transfers: surface steps need explicit drying or mineral context, hydrolyses need aqueous pH, temperature, or catalytic justification, the hydantoin branch is weaker than the Strecker branch, and the formamide-to-HCN step is plausible but relatively harsh. If biochemical is intended literally, missing enzymes or catalysts should be named; if not, relabel those nodes as aqueous hydrolysis. Broadly consistent with known chemistry, but explicit literature citations are absent.\n- Issues: ['Hydantoin is a legitimate protected precursor, but the net Bucherer-Bergs-type formation is more condition-sensitive than the Strecker branch and is not well matched to a generic surface label. rxn_009 also needs 2 H2O stoichiometrically and usually stronger or stepwise hydrolysis conditions.', 'This is the weakest route because it combines the harsh formamide-to-HCN step with the already condition-sensitive hydantoin branch. rxn_008 needs better environmental justification, and rxn_009 should be written with 2 H2O and stronger hydrolytic conditions.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.82\n- Feedback: Network validity is high: the graph is connected, molecule IDs are consistent, and the deterministic balance check reports no conservation-violating reactions. The chemically strongest core is the aminoacetonitrile hub reached by direct or imine-resolved Strecker chemistry, with P1 best and P3 close behind. Confidence drops because rxn_008 and rxn_011 should explicitly consume 2 H2O, rxn_006 and rxn_007 are chemically plausible but environmentally misassigned as 'Biochemical', rxn_005 is only moderately justified, and rxn_010 is the least defensible step in the network. Hydantoin chemistry is appropriately treated as a secondary, condition-restricted branch rather than the main route. Overall, this is a good but not approval-grade network; clearer condition specification, explicit material-transfer justifications, and stronger support or pruning of the weaker branches, especially rxn_005 and rxn_010, would improve confidence.\n- Issues: [\"The chemistry is mechanistically sensible because nitrile hydrolysis commonly proceeds through an amide, but the stated environment is the major weakness. rxn_006 and rxn_007 are not convincingly supported as prebiotic 'Biochemical' steps unless they are reinterpreted as hot aqueous or mineral-catalyzed hydrolysis.\", 'This pathway is coherent on paper, but its confidence is limited by the same environment problem as P2. The Strecker segment is reasonable, yet rxn_006 and rxn_007 need prebiotic hydrolysis conditions rather than an undefined biochemical setting.', 'Glycolonitrile formation is strong, but the weakest point here is rxn_005. Direct conversion of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile by NH3 is much less secure than the Strecker branches and likely needs specific activation, catalysis, or drying chemistry. Hydrothermal hydrolysis to glycine is still plausible once aminoacetonitrile is available, with rxn_008 needing 2 H2O stoichiometry.', 'This branch inherits two distinct weaknesses: rxn_005 is only moderately convincing, and the glycinamide hydrolysis sequence is placed in an inadequately defined biochemical environment. The pathway can reach glycine conceptually, but the environmental specification is not yet prebiotically coherent.', \"Hydantoin chemistry can in principle furnish glycine, but this branch is appropriately weaker than the aminonitrile routes. rxn_009 likely requires concentrated ammonium carbonate or carbamate chemistry and possibly drying phases beyond what the simple 'Surface' label conveys, and rxn_011 is plausible only under fairly strong hydrothermal hydrolysis. rxn_011 should also be written with 2 H2O.\", 'The detour through hydantoin is the main problem. rxn_010, carboxylative cyclization of glycinamide to hydantoin from CO2 alone, is under-justified and likely needs a more activated carbamylating chemistry or much tighter condition control. The pathway also depends on the poorly specified biochemical environment used in rxn_006 and on stoichiometric correction of rxn_011.', 'Although the early Strecker chemistry is reasonable, the pathway becomes weak once it diverts to hydantoin through rxn_010. The network does not adequately justify glycinamide carboxylative cyclization from CO2 alone, and the environment sequence from surface to biochemical to surface to hydrothermal is not physically explained.', 'This is the weakest route in the network because it combines the uncertain glycolonitrile-to-aminoacetonitrile conversion with the poorly justified glycinamide-to-hydantoin cyclization. The pathway is also environmentally diffuse, with multiple unsupported transitions and only a harshly plausible final hydantoin hydrolysis.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network is organized around aminoacetonitrile as a robust hub intermediate. Surface-accessible HCN is supplied either by reduced-gas photochemistry or discharge chemistry, or more restrictively by formamide dehydration in drying geothermal films. Formaldehyde and ammonia then enter one-pot, imine-resolved, or ammonium-cyanide-buffered Strecker manifolds to give aminoacetonitrile. Because aminonitriles are more persistent than free amino acids, aminoacetonitrile can plausibly bridge surface cyanide chemistry and hot aqueous hydrothermal hydrolysis, yielding glycine either stepwise through glycinamide or by the net two-water hydrolysis of the nitrile. Ten explicit pathways arise from combining these source, assembly, and hydrolysis branches, while weaker hydantoin and glycolonitrile amination routes are intentionally omitted.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH3NO/c2-1-3/h1H,(H2,2,3)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanimine\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonium cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonium cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoethanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoethanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoethanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduced-gas photochemical cyanide formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic reduced volcanic plume or gas-water interface; UV and/or electrical discharge provides activation and HCN is trapped in adjacent surface waters\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"High-energy radical photochemistry or discharge chemistry dehydrogenates methane and ammonia to CN-bearing fragments that collapse to HCN, with H2 as the reductive coproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"HCN is a canonical product of UV or discharge processing of reduced C-N gases, and local volcanic or impact-reduced niches can supply it directly to nearby pools (Miller, 1953; Stribling and Miller, 1987). This provides a strong surface cyanide source without invoking unsupported catalytic biology.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide dehydration to hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\", \"alumina\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica/alumina mineral surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Low-water geothermal film or crust; repeated drying; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-assisted dehydration of formamide eliminates water to produce HCN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Heating formamide on mineral surfaces can access HCN-rich chemistry, but this branch is more condition-restricted and lower-confidence than reduced-gas photochemical HCN formation, so it is included only as a secondary route (Barks et al., 2010; Saladino et al., 2012).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cyanide-bearing pool or evaporitic film; wet-dry concentration; pH about 6-9; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"In situ imine or iminium formation from formaldehyde and ammonia is followed by cyanide addition and net dehydration to the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the strongest prebiotic entry to the glycine manifold: direct Strecker chemistry from formaldehyde gives aminoacetonitrile, the canonical C2 precursor to glycine (Miller, 1957; Sanchez et al., 1966). Surface concentration helps overcome dilution and compete with cyanide and formaldehyde side reactions better than fully dilute water-column chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-ammonia condensation to methanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Drying mineral film or evaporitic interface; anoxic; water removal favors imine accumulation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of formaldehyde and ammonia with loss of water generates methanimine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Resolving Strecker chemistry into an explicit imine step reflects the accepted mechanism. Drying interfaces are chemically justified because methanimine formation is disfavored in dilute bulk water (Sanchez et al., 1966; Cleaves, 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide addition to methanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Rehydrated cyanide-bearing film or concentrated pool; pH about 6-9; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic cyanide addition to methanimine or its protonated iminium form yields aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This stepwise imine-plus-cyanide branch is mechanistically strong and stays within canonical Strecker chemistry, avoiding the weaker glycolonitrile amination branch criticized in earlier network designs (Sanchez et al., 1966; Danger et al., 2012).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonium cyanide reservoir formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich cyanide solution or brine; acid-base equilibrium reservoir in anoxic surface waters\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proton transfer and ion pairing convert dissolved HCN and NH3 into ammonium cyanide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ammonium cyanide is a realistic buffered reservoir of cyanide in ammonia-rich prebiotic solutions and is well represented in cyanide polymerization and prebiotic synthesis studies (Oro and Kimball, 1961; Ferris et al., 1978).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonium cyanide Strecker-equivalent synthesis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammonium cyanide solution or drying film; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Buffered Strecker-equivalent addition of cyanide to the formaldehyde-ammonia adduct gives aminoacetonitrile with net water release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Representing ammonium cyanide as the operative cyanide donor captures ammonia-buffered Strecker chemistry without invoking unsupported side branches. It is chemically closer to canonical Strecker synthesis than direct amination of glycolonitrile (Miller, 1957; Sanchez et al., 1966).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Partial hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hot anoxic water in mineral pores or subseafloor hydrothermal fluids; pH about 5-8; partial hydrolysis favored at shorter residence time\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydration of the nitrile carbon followed by tautomerization gives the corresponding amide, glycinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitriles are widely treated as kinetically accessible prebiotic reservoirs that hydrolyze stepwise in hot water. Explicit hydrothermal placement gives the aqueous pH and temperature context missing from vague biochemical labels and makes glycinamide a justified partial-hydrolysis node (Commeyras et al., 2009; Danger et al., 2012).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glycinamide to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-450K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Longer residence in hot anoxic water; pH about 5-8; likely slower than rxn_008\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hot-water amide hydrolysis converts glycinamide to glycine and liberates ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is slower than the initial nitrile hydration and is therefore a plausible bottleneck, but it remains chemically reasonable in hot compressed water without enzymes. Writing this explicitly as hot aqueous hydrolysis avoids an ill-defined biochemical conversion (Commeyras et al., 2009; Danger et al., 2012).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Net hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-450K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hot compressed anoxic water; net stoichiometry consumes two water equivalents and glycinamide may remain only transiently detectable\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Successive nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis give the overall two-water conversion of aminoacetonitrile to glycine with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The direct aminoacetonitrile-to-glycine step is a coarse-grained net hydrolysis that explicitly requires two water equivalents and likely passes through glycinamide in situ. Representing the net conversion is justified when the amide does not accumulate, and hydrothermal water provides the necessary abiotic hydrolytic drive (Commeyras et al., 2009; Danger et al., 2012).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical HCN, one-pot Strecker, net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Reduced-gas surface photochemistry supplies HCN, which enters direct Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile followed by net hydrothermal hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical HCN, one-pot Strecker, stepwise hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same high-confidence surface cyanide source feeds one-pot Strecker chemistry, but hydrolysis is resolved through glycinamide before glycine appears.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical HCN, imine-resolved Strecker, net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN from reduced-gas photochemistry is combined with explicit methanimine formation before cyanide capture and net hydrothermal hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical HCN, imine-resolved Strecker, stepwise hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The imine-resolved Strecker branch is followed by partial nitrile hydrolysis to glycinamide and then amide hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical HCN, ammonium cyanide route, net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN is first buffered as ammonium cyanide, which then acts as the operative cyanide donor in a Strecker-equivalent route to aminoacetonitrile before net hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical HCN, ammonium cyanide route, stepwise hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The buffered ammonium cyanide branch reaches aminoacetonitrile and then proceeds through glycinamide under hydrothermal hydrolysis conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide HCN, one-pot Strecker, net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A lower-confidence geothermal surface branch uses formamide dehydration as the HCN source, then proceeds by direct Strecker capture and net hydrothermal hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide HCN, one-pot Strecker, stepwise hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Formamide-derived HCN feeds the one-pot Strecker branch, and aminoacetonitrile is then hydrolyzed stepwise through glycinamide.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide HCN, imine-resolved Strecker, net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A secondary formamide-based cyanide source is combined with explicit methanimine formation before cyanide addition and net hydrothermal hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide HCN, imine-resolved Strecker, stepwise hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The most explicit lower-confidence formamide branch resolves both aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis, passing through methanimine and glycinamide before glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "The network is organized around aminoacetonitrile as a robust hub intermediate. Surface-accessible HCN is supplied either by reduced-gas photochemistry or discharge chemistry, or more restrictively by formamide dehydration in drying geothermal films. Formaldehyde and ammonia then enter one-pot, imine-resolved, or ammonium-cyanide-buffered Strecker manifolds to give aminoacetonitrile. Because aminonitriles are more persistent than free amino acids, aminoacetonitrile can plausibly bridge surface cyanide chemistry and hot aqueous hydrothermal hydrolysis, yielding glycine either stepwise through glycinamide or by the net two-water hydrolysis of the nitrile. Ten explicit pathways arise from combining these source, assembly, and hydrolysis branches, while weaker hydantoin and glycolonitrile amination routes are intentionally omitted.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH3NO", "iupac_name": "methanamide", "common_name": "formamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH3NO/c2-1-3/h1H,(H2,2,3)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "CH3N", "iupac_name": "methanimine", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "CH4N2", "iupac_name": "ammonium cyanide", "common_name": "ammonium cyanide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanenitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C2H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanamide", "common_name": "glycinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanoic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Reduced-gas photochemical cyanide formation", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surfa