anide reservoir formation", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich cyanide solution or brine; acid-base equilibrium reservoir in anoxic surface waters"}, "mechanism": "Proton transfer and ion pairing convert dissolved HCN and NH3 into ammonium cyanide.", "reasoning": "Ammonium cyanide is a realistic buffered reservoir of cyanide in ammonia-rich prebiotic solutions and is well represented in cyanide polymerization and prebiotic synthesis studies (Oro and Kimball, 1961; Ferris et al., 1978)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Ammonium cyanide Strecker-equivalent synthesis", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammonium cyanide solution or drying film; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Buffered Strecker-equivalent addition of cyanide to the formaldehyde-ammonia adduct gives aminoacetonitrile with net water release.", "reasoning": "Representing ammonium cyanide as the operative cyanide donor captures ammonia-buffered Strecker chemistry without invoking unsupported side branches. It is chemically closer to canonical Strecker synthesis than direct amination of glycolonitrile (Miller, 1957; Sanchez et al., 1966)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Partial hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycinamide", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "370-420K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Hot anoxic water in mineral pores or subseafloor hydrothermal fluids; pH about 5-8; partial hydrolysis favored at shorter residence time"}, "mechanism": "Hydration of the nitrile carbon followed by tautomerization gives the corresponding amide, glycinamide.", "reasoning": "Aminonitriles are widely treated as kinetically accessible prebiotic reservoirs that hydrolyze stepwise in hot water. Explicit hydrothermal placement gives the aqueous pH and temperature context missing from vague biochemical labels and makes glycinamide a justified partial-hydrolysis node (Commeyras et al., 2009; Danger et al., 2012)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydrolysis of glycinamide to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "390-450K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Longer residence in hot anoxic water; pH about 5-8; likely slower than rxn_008"}, "mechanism": "Hot-water amide hydrolysis converts glycinamide to glycine and liberates ammonia.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is slower than the initial nitrile hydration and is therefore a plausible bottleneck, but it remains chemically reasonable in hot compressed water without enzymes. Writing this explicitly as hot aqueous hydrolysis avoids an ill-defined biochemical conversion (Commeyras et al., 2009; Danger et al., 2012)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Net hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "390-450K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Hot compressed anoxic water; net stoichiometry consumes two water equivalents and glycinamide may remain only transiently detectable"}, "mechanism": "Successive nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis give the overall two-water conversion of aminoacetonitrile to glycine with ammonia release.", "reasoning": "The direct aminoacetonitrile-to-glycine step is a coarse-grained net hydrolysis that explicitly requires two water equivalents and likely passes through glycinamide in situ. Representi